{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260916140721b77e17e55d584c98","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I see the key logic. At line ~6227:\n\n```go\nfor i, a := range args {\n    paramIndex := i + paramOffset\n    if paramIndex < len(target.paramNames) {\n        pnode := target.params[target.paramNames[paramIndex]]\n        l.flow(a, pnode)\n        ...\n        mapped[i] = true\n    }\n}\n```\n\nSo an arg maps to a param only if `paramIndex < len(target.paramNames)`. For a variadic C call:\n\n```c\nvoid log_msg(int level, const char *fmt, ...) { ... }\n\nlog_msg(LOG_INFO, \"%s: %s\", name, tainted_value);\n```\n\nThe callee `log_msg` has params `[\"level\", \"fmt\"]` (the `...` is dropped by the frontend — cMacroParamList drops variadic tails for macros; what about function definitions?). The call has 4 args. Args at index 2, 3 exceed `len(target.paramNames)` = 2, so they don't flow into any param — `mapped[i]` stays false and they get the conservative `arg → result` edge. So taint from `tainted_value` reaches the call's *result*, but `log_msg` returns void... and the flow never enters the callee's body.\n\nThe sink for the CVE is presumably inside the wrapper's body (e.g. `vfprintf`/`vsyslog`/`fprintf(stderr, ...)`), so the flow is severed: the taint stays at the call boundary (arg node) and never reaches the wrapper's body where the actual logging call is.\n\nThe gap title says: \"taint is not traced into a variadic C function call, so a printf-style logging wrapper blocks every flow at its own call boundary\".\n\nSo the fix: when a C (or C++) function's declaration is variadic, the frontend should record that, and lowering should route the excess args into some node that reaches the body — probably the callee's `va_list`-derived reads, or more simply a synthetic \"varargs\" param node that the body's `va_arg`/vprintf reads flow from.\n\nHmm, but what does the body actually look like?\n\n```c\nvoid log_msg(int level, const char *fmt, ...) {\n    va_list ap;\n    va_start(ap, fmt);\n    vfprintf(log_file, fmt, ap);\n    va_end(ap);\n}\n```\n\nThe tainted argument is in `ap`. For the flow to reach `vfprintf`, the engine would need to model va_list. That's a big lift. Alternatively, maybe the definition's sink is the wrapper itself (the call to `log_msg`), and the issue is different.\n\nWait — re-read the gap: \"taint is not traced into a variadic C function call, so a printf-style logging wrapper blocks every flow at its own call boundary\". \"blocks every flow at its own call boundary\" — the wrapper blocks (i.e., stops) every flow at its own call boundary. So flows into the wrapper don't continue into the body. The missing behaviour: trace taint INTO the variadic call.\n\nLet me think about what the AIDE CVE looks like. AIDE: CVE-2024-33187? Hmm. Let me think — \"control char log output\". There's a real AIDE vulnerability: AIDE logs filenames containing control characters (e.g., terminal escape sequences) which can be used to corrupt terminal output or manipulate log viewers. The fix sanitized control characters before logging. In AIDE source, there's a wrapper... e.g.\n\n```c\nvoid log_msg(int level, const char *format, ...) {\n    va_list ap;\n    va_start(ap, format);\n    ...\n    vfprintf(stderr, format, ap);  // or syslog(level, format, ap) via vsyslog\n}\n```\n\nAnd the vulnerable flow: attacker-controlled filename → `log_msg(LOG_LEVEL, \"file %s\", fname)` → unsanitized output to log.\n\nThe definition would name a sink like `fprintf`/`vfprintf`/`syslog` inside... but the definitions side may instead name the wrapper itself as the sink concept with a binding on `log_msg`-style variadic wrappers? I can't see the definitions. But the gap statement tells me exactly what the engine cannot do: trace taint into a variadic C function call.\n\nHmm wait, but there's another reading. Consider the *caller* side: a printf-style logging wrapper like:\n\n```c\n#define LOG(level, fmt, ...) log_msg(level, fmt, __VA_ARGS__)\n```\n\nNo — \"blocks every flow at its own call boundary\" — the wrapper's own call boundary.\n\nLet me look at how the test file name describes it: `cve_rank3007_aide_control_char_log_output`. So the flow is source (attacker data, e.g. filename from filesystem scan) → log output sink, unsanitized control characters. The flow passes through `log_msg(...)` variadic wrapper.\n\nFor the engine, the most natural minimal change: when the frontend lowers a C function definition whose parameter list ends with `...`, record a synthetic variadic parameter (e.g. `\"__varargs__\"`), so excess call arguments flow into it, and inside the body, `va_start`/`va_arg`/`va_list` reads flow from it. But modeling va_list usage in the body requires the body to actually read the varargs through something the frontend can see.\n\nLet me look at what the C frontend does with `va_list`, `va_start`, `va_arg` currently.","signature":"9b52fbd5d194463f9a230c95"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2e47c8b-b052-45f8-b9f8-606406eb025a","uuid":"1d51cb1c-958a-4a09-862c-86091db55c25","timestamp":"2026-09-16T06:07:40.423Z"}
