{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260916140754fd1d3f44966e481c","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Very helpful. Bindings name sinks on call args (e.g. `emit sink code.FormatString at args[0]`). \"printf, fprintf, syslog and vprintf already do [carry code.FormatString] in the generated C bindings\".\n\nNow for rank 3007 (aide control char log output): The likely shape is a sink at a logging call inside a wrapper, and the source is attacker-controlled data (e.g., a filename from the filesystem). The flow must go:\n\n```c\n// somewhere in aide\nlog_msg(LOG_LEVEL_INFO, \"Processing %s\", filename);   // filename is tainted\n\n// log.c\nvoid log_msg(int msg_level, const char *format, ...) {\n    va_start(ap, format);\n    ...\n    vsyslog(msg_level, format, ap);  // or vfprintf\n}\n```\n\nHmm wait, but if the sink is `code.FormatString` at a printf-family format arg... no, that's for format string injection, not control char output.\n\nActually let me think about what \"control char log output\" is: the vulnerability is that attacker-controlled data (file names scanned by AIDE) containing control characters is written to the log unsanitized. The definition likely names a sink like `code.LogOutput` or unsanitized output... The precise definition is the rank's job; mine is the engine behaviour: \"taint is not traced into a variadic C function call\".\n\nSo: taint reaching an argument in the variadic tail (beyond the named params) of a call to a locally-defined C function never enters that function's body. The fix should route those excess arguments into the callee's body — but where in the body? The natural target: a synthetic variadic parameter, and then the body's reads of it — `va_arg`, or the `va_list` handed to `vprintf`/`vfprintf`/`vsyslog` — should flow from that synthetic param.\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider: maybe the sink is *at the wrapper's own call site* — e.g. the definition names `log_msg`'s variadic tail args as the sink (`emit sink code.X at args[2..]`)? But bindings can name args by position. If the wrapper is `log_msg(level, fmt, ...)`, the binding could emit a sink at args[2] — but a binding is per-library; for aide's own `log_msg`, the rank's binding would name it. Then taint would reach the arg node at the call site and the sink would fire without any engine change...\n\nUnless: the sink is inside the wrapper (the actual write to stderr/log file, e.g. `vfprintf`), and the rule wants source→sink through the wrapper. The gap says \"a printf-style logging wrapper blocks every flow at its own call boundary\" — the wrapper blocks flows at ITS OWN call boundary, i.e., flows into the wrapper stop at the boundary. So the sink is inside or beyond the wrapper.\n\nSo the engine change: route variadic-tail arguments into the callee body. Two levels:\n\n1. Give the FuncDef a synthetic param representing the `...` tail so `target.paramNames` covers excess args (mapped), flowing into a param node.\n2. Make the body's uses of the va_list (va_start/va_arg, or the va_list passed to v-printf family) read from that param node.\n\nActually wait. Let me look at how the C frontend lowers the body of such a wrapper. `va_list ap; va_start(ap, fmt); vfprintf(f, fmt, ap);`. The frontend lowers `va_start(ap, fmt)` as a call with args `ap` (a Name, maybe undeclared local) and `fmt`. `vfprintf(log_file, fmt, ap)` is a call with three args. If `ap`'s value node were connected to the synthetic varargs param node, taint would flow: arg at call site → varargs param → ap → vfprintf's args[2] (an Arg slot at the vfprintf call — where a binding can place a sink, or the vfprintf's `va_list` arg...).\n\nHmm, but actually for the sink at `vfprintf(f, fmt, ap)`, the sink concept would be on the format string or the ap? The FormatString sink is args[1] (fmt). The tainted control chars flow through `ap`.\n\nLet me check how the C frontend handles `va_start` — is there any special-casing? Let me search the C frontend for how it treats unknown calls and whether there's any va_list handling at all. Also let me check the generated C bindings for printf-family sinks. Where are the generated C bindings? \"printf, fprintf, syslog and vprintf already do [carry code.FormatString] in the generated C bindings\" — those are in internal/bindings? Let me look.","signature":"aeaa983e09124c7791644b42"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2e47c8b-b052-45f8-b9f8-606406eb025a","uuid":"0875d458-8a83-4c4c-a1a1-74bbde681a5d","timestamp":"2026-09-16T06:08:13.984Z"}
