{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_ea83af5357d24d8291b291b7","type":"tool_result","content":"# Rank 2019 / CVE-2026-33210 (CWE-134, fixed in json 2.15.2.1):\n# JSON.parse(doc, allow_duplicate_key: false) parses the document in the C\n# extension, and when an object carries a duplicate key, the key's own bytes\n# reach a format string. The vulnerable chain, all inside\n# ext/json/ext/parser/parser.c at the parent of e26694b:\n#\n#   static void raise_duplicate_key_error(JSON_ParserState *state, VALUE duplicate_key)\n#   {\n#       VALUE message = rb_sprintf(\"duplicate key %\"PRIsVALUE, rb_inspect(duplicate_key));\n#       raise_parse_error(RSTRING_PTR(message), state);   // <-- the key, as a format\n#       RB_GC_GUARD(message);\n#   }\n#\n#   static void raise_parse_error(const char *format, JSON_ParserState *state)\n#   {\n#       ...\n#       VALUE msg = rb_sprintf(format, ptr);              // <-- parsed key bytes format\n#       ...\n#   }\n#\n# duplicate_key is a key parsed out of the document, so a key named \"%s%s%s%s\"\n# puts its conversions into rb_sprintf's format argument: each %s binds an\n# argument the call never passed and %n writes through one. The fix routes the\n# rendered message around the format slot (build_parse_error_message with a \"\"\n# format plus rb_str_concat) instead of through it.\n#\n# The taint route is genuinely unreachable to the scanner: no labelled source\n# reaches the format slot at either revision (`vyql trace -to FormatString`\n# reports 0 reachable pairs, 412 dead-ending sources -- the entry params are\n# Ruby-level and the parse machinery between them and the key is Ragel state,\n# and the fix's own helper still receives its format as a parameter). What is\n# visible without dataflow is CWE-134's precondition, the one -Wformat-nonliteral\n# checks and the one PRINTF_ARGS declares: the format argument of a\n# formatted-output call is a value the program computed, not a literal its\n# author wrote. That observation is what ships: the rb_* printf family named by\n# ruby.h (rb_sprintf/rb_vsprintf at arg 0, rb_enc_sprintf/rb_enc_vsprintf and\n# rb_raise one in, rb_fatal/rb_bug/rb_warn/rb_warning at arg 0) as\n# code.FormatString sinks, plus code.NonliteralFormatString/VYQL-INJ-284 on a\n# computed shape (ID or *ID) in one of those slots.\n#\n# The report lands on raise_parse_error -- the CVE's own sink -- and stays\n# there after the fix, because the fix stops the key reaching the helper and\n# leaves the helper's computed format in place; a caller-supplied literal is\n# invisible to a shape that cannot see callers. Both revisions scan to 246\n# findings with exactly one VYQL-INJ-284 (parser.c:434 at the parent,\n# parser.c:431 in build_parse_error_message at the fix) and the base to 245\n# with none; the fingerprint diff is that one row added, nothing moved.\n#\n# The cases below mirror the repository's code. The first is the vulnerable\n# pair with the real signatures and the intermediate assignments (ptr starts\n# as \"EOF\" and becomes the quoted fragment; message is built before it is\n# handed over). The second is the same two functions under the control the\n# advisory names -- format with a literal, pass the computed value as an\n# argument -- which is the shape the fix moved to. The third pins the two\n# forms that must stay silent: the variadic forwarding wrapper Ruby's own\n# headers declare RBIMPL_ATTR_FORMAT (va_start plus rb_vsprintf, the\n# documented way to add a formatted-error helper to an extension), and the\n# everyday literal rb_raise with a computed argument.\n\ntest \"rank2019: a duplicate key's bytes reach rb_sprintf as the format\"\n  lang c\n  profile library\n  file ext/json/ext/parser/parser.c\n  ```\n#include <ruby.h>\n\n#define PARSE_ERROR_FRAGMENT_LEN 32\n\nstatic rb_encoding *enc_utf8;\n\nstatic void cursor_position(JSON_ParserState *state, long *line_out, long *column_out)\n{\n    const char *cursor = state->cursor;\n    long column = 0;\n    long line = 1;\n\n    while (cursor >= state->start) {\n        if (*cursor-- == '\\n') {\n            break;\n        }\n        column++;\n    }\n    *line_out = line;\n    *column_out = column;\n}\n\nstatic void raise_parse_error(const char *format, JSON_ParserState *state)\n{\n    unsigned char buffer[PARSE_ERROR_FRAGMENT_LEN + 3];\n    long line, column;\n    cursor_position(state, &line, &column);\n\n    const char *ptr = \"EOF\";\n    if (state->cursor && state->cursor < state->end) {\n        ptr = state->cursor;\n        size_t len = 0;\n        while (len < PARSE_ERROR_FRAGMENT_LEN) {\n            char ch = ptr[len];\n            if (!ch || ch == '\\n' || ch == ' ' || ch == '\\t' || ch == '\\r') {\n                break;\n            }\n            len++;\n        }\n        if (len) {\n            buffer[0] = '\\'';\n            MEMCPY(buffer + 1, ptr, char, len);\n            buffer[len + 1] = '\\'';\n            buffer[len + 2] = '\\0';\n            ptr = (const char *)buffer;\n        }\n    }\n\n    VALUE msg = rb_sprintf(format, ptr);\n    VALUE message = rb_enc_sprintf(enc_utf8, \"%s at line %ld column %ld\", RSTRING_PTR(msg), line, column);\n    RB_GC_GUARD(msg);\n\n    VALUE exc = rb_exc_new_str(rb_path2class(\"JSON::ParserError\"), message);\n    rb_ivar_set(exc, rb_intern(\"@line\"), LONG2NUM(line));\n    rb_exc_raise(exc);\n}\n\nstatic void raise_duplicate_key_error(JSON_ParserState *state, VALUE duplicate_key)\n{\n    VALUE message = rb_sprintf(\n        \"duplicate key %\"PRIsVALUE,\n        rb_inspect(duplicate_key)\n    );\n\n    raise_parse_error(RSTRING_PTR(message), state);\n    RB_GC_GUARD(message);\n}\n  ```\n  expect VYQL-INJ-284\n\n\ntest \"rank2019: the rendered key as an argument of a literal format stays silent\"\n  lang c\n  profile library\n  file ext/json/ext/parser/parser.c\n  ```\n#include <ruby.h>\n\n#define PARSE_ERROR_FRAGMENT_LEN 32\n\nstatic rb_encoding *enc_utf8;\n\nstatic void cursor_position(JSON_ParserState *state, long *line_out, long *column_out)\n{\n    const char *cursor = state->cursor;\n    long column = 0;\n    long line = 1;\n\n    while (cursor >= state->start) {\n        if (*cursor-- == '\\n') {\n            break;\n        }\n        column++;\n    }\n    *line_out = line;\n    *column_out = column;\n}\n\nstatic void raise_parse_error(const char *fragment, JSON_ParserState *state)\n{\n    unsigned char buffer[PARSE_ERROR_FRAGMENT_LEN + 3];\n    long line, column;\n    cursor_position(state, &line, &column);\n\n    const char *ptr = \"EOF\";\n    if (state->cursor && state->cursor < state->end) {\n        ptr = state->cursor;\n        size_t len = 0;\n        while (len < PARSE_ERROR_FRAGMENT_LEN) {\n            char ch = ptr[len];\n            if (!ch || ch == '\\n' || ch == ' ' || ch == '\\t' || ch == '\\r') {\n                break;\n            }\n            len++;\n        }\n        if (len) {\n            buffer[0] = '\\'';\n            MEMCPY(buffer + 1, ptr, char, len);\n            buffer[len + 1] = '\\'';\n            buffer[len + 2] = '\\0';\n            ptr = (const char *)buffer;\n        }\n    }\n\n    VALUE msg = rb_sprintf(\"%s: %s\", fragment, ptr);\n    VALUE message = rb_enc_sprintf(enc_utf8, \"%s at line %ld column %ld\", RSTRING_PTR(msg), line, column);\n    RB_GC_GUARD(msg);\n\n    VALUE exc = rb_exc_new_str(rb_path2class(\"JSON::ParserError\"), message);\n    rb_ivar_set(exc, rb_intern(\"@line\"), LONG2NUM(line));\n    rb_exc_raise(exc);\n}\n\nstatic void raise_duplicate_key_error(JSON_ParserState *state, VALUE duplicate_key)\n{\n    VALUE message = rb_sprintf(\n        \"duplicate key %\"PRIsVALUE,\n        rb_inspect(duplicate_key)\n    );\n\n    raise_parse_error(RSTRING_PTR(message), state);\n    RB_GC_GUARD(message);\n}\n  ```\n  reject VYQL-INJ-284\n\n\ntest \"rank2019: the forwarding wrapper and the literal rb_raise stay silent\"\n  lang c\n  profile library\n  file ext/json/ext/generator/generator.c\n  ```\n#include <ruby.h>\n\nstatic void raise_generator_error_str(VALUE invalid_object, VALUE str)\n{\n    VALUE exc = rb_exc_new_str(eGeneratorError, str);\n    rb_ivar_set(exc, rb_intern(\"@invalid_object\"), invalid_object);\n    rb_exc_raise(exc);\n}\n\nstatic void raise_generator_error(VALUE invalid_object, const char *fmt, ...)\n{\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, fmt);\n    VALUE str = rb_vsprintf(fmt, args);\n    va_end(args);\n    raise_generator_error_str(invalid_object, str);\n}\n\nstatic void raise_nesting_error(JSON_ParserState *state)\n{\n    rb_raise(eNestingError, \"nesting of %d is too deep\", state->current_nesting);\n}\n  ```\n  reject VYQL-INJ-284","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2e47c8b-b052-45f8-b9f8-606406eb025a","uuid":"c53dab62-0185-4f57-95b1-fc6da7b4cdcd","timestamp":"2026-09-16T06:08:38.838Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Rank 2019 / CVE-2026-33210 (CWE-134, fixed in json 2.15.2.1):\n# JSON.parse(doc, allow_duplicate_key: false) parses the document in the C\n# extension, and when an object carries a duplicate key, the key's own bytes\n# reach a format string. The vulnerable chain, all inside\n# ext/json/ext/parser/parser.c at the parent of e26694b:\n#\n#   static void raise_duplicate_key_error(JSON_ParserState *state, VALUE duplicate_key)\n#   {\n#       VALUE message = rb_sprintf(\"duplicate key %\"PRIsVALUE, rb_inspect(duplicate_key));\n#       raise_parse_error(RSTRING_PTR(message), state);   // <-- the key, as a format\n#       RB_GC_GUARD(message);\n#   }\n#\n#   static void raise_parse_error(const char *format, JSON_ParserState *state)\n#   {\n#       ...\n#       VALUE msg = rb_sprintf(format, ptr);              // <-- parsed key bytes format\n#       ...\n#   }\n#\n# duplicate_key is a key parsed out of the document, so a key named \"%s%s%s%s\"\n# puts its conversions into rb_sprintf's format argument: each %s binds an\n# argument the call never passed and %n writes through one. The fix routes the\n# rend