c tail param, route every arg at paramIndex >= len(named params)... wait, if the synthetic param is IN paramNames, then arg i maps for i < len(paramNames). Only the first excess arg maps. Need: map ALL args from the tail position onward into the varargs param node. So lowering needs to know which param is the synthetic tail. Could mark it by name: `nir.CVarargsParam = \"__varargs__\"`. In the mapping loop:\n\n```go\nfor i, a := range args {\n    paramIndex := i + paramOffset\n    if paramIndex < len(target.paramNames) {\n        ... existing ...\n    } else if vp := target.params[nir.CVarargsParam]; vp != \"\" && target.variadic {\n        l.flow(a, vp)\n        mapped[i] = true\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHmm wait, but there's a subtlety: if the synthetic param is appended to `paramNames`, then `paramIndex < len(paramNames)` maps tail arg 0 to it via the normal path, and tail args 1..n need the extra branch. Simpler: keep the synthetic name OUT of `paramNames` order but in `params` map? `paramNames` is `order` built from `st.Params` — makeFuncInfo iterates st.Params to build both. I could instead not append the synthetic to st.Params in the FuncDef but record variadic-ness on the FuncDef (`Variadic bool`), create the param node in makeFuncInfo when st.Variadic, and map excess args into it in the call loop. And in the body, `va_start(ap, ...)` → `ap = __varargs__` assignment; the frontend knows the function is variadic (it's lowering the same declaration) so it can emit `Name(\"__varargs__\")`, and the lowering's function scope must bind that name to the synthetic Param node — which it will if makeFuncInfo creates the node and the scope setup includes it. Let me check how the function scope binds params (in `stmt` FuncDef handling around line 4102).\n\nAlso consider: `mapped[i] = true` for tail args means they no longer get the conservative `arg → result` edge. Is that right? For a void logging wrapper, the result is unused; for something like `vsprintf`-returning wrappers, the return isn't the tail args anyway. But conservative edge loss could reduce recall in cases where the callee is variadic and unresolved... no wait — targets are resolved local functions; the in-body route is strictly more precise. But if the wrapper's body doesn't actually read the tail (e.g. `va_start` never modeled because the body uses `__builtin_va_start` or macros), we'd lose the arg→result edge. Keep it safe: still mark mapped only when we actually flow into the param. Flowing into the param node is real, but if nothing in the body reads the param, taint dies. Hmm — the conservative edge arg→result exists for unresolved callees. For a *resolved* callee, the current behaviour for args within param count is: flow into param, mapped=true, no arg→result edge. The body might ignore the param — that's the precision contract of resolved calls (\"an in-body transform is honoured\"). So treating the varargs tail the same way is consistent. But to be careful about blast radius, maybe keep `mapped[i] = true` only if the flow goes into the varargs param — which it does. It's the same discipline as named params. OK.\n\nBut wait — is there also a va_list read path that doesn't go through va_start? `va_arg(ap, T)` is an expression reading the next tail element. If a body uses `va_arg` directly without forwarding to a v-function, taint would need to flow from `ap`. If `va_start(ap, fmt)` gives `ap` the varargs taint, then `va_arg(ap, ...)` reads `ap` — normal Name eval flows. Good.\n\nAlso `va_copy(dst, src)`: `dst = src` aliasing.\n\nNow, what about *C++*? The gap says C. tree-sitter-cpp also has variadic_parameter. The c.go converter handles both C and C++ (ccConv). Since the same frontend file handles cpp, the change might automatically apply to C++ too. That's within blast radius to note.\n\nNow — hold on. Before designing, let me confirm the actual current behaviour with a test. Let me check the existing tests for the C frontend around calls and taint: `c_member_taint_