{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026091614120665703d5cb0e74a5d","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The CVE is confirmed: filenames with terminal control characters reach report and log output unescaped. The flow: filename (from filesystem scan) → log_msg(...) variadic → output.\n\nThe engine behaviour needed: taint through the variadic call boundary. My design holds regardless of the exact sink function inside the wrapper (vfprintf/vsyslog/etc.) — what matters is the tail argument reaching *inside* the callee's body through the `va_list`.\n\nNow, an important design question: where does the tail taint go inside the body? The wrapper does `va_start(ap, format); vfprintf(f, format, ap);`. The natural modelling: `va_start(ap, X)` binds `ap` to the variadic tail. Then `ap`'s value node carries tail taint into any call that receives it (like vfprintf's arg) and into `va_arg` reads.\n\nLet me now verify the tree-sitter node kind for `...`. Let me write a quick dump.","signature":"a9136d1a4a494a6ca9c6e76f"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2e47c8b-b052-45f8-b9f8-606406eb025a","uuid":"94597e20-fc6c-4025-bb86-b8f524f5ad49","timestamp":"2026-09-16T06:12:13.173Z"}
