{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202609161413308aa4c5b10e244353","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The param-source fallback only labels `exported == \"true\"` params (public API entry points). The synthetic tail param would have `exported` set if `st.Exported` is true and I put it through the same path in makeFuncInfo (`if st.Exported { props[\"exported\"] = \"true\" }` — that applies to ALL params in the loop). A variadic public entry point's tail being labelled an entry source — that's semantically right (printf's `...` in a public API is external input? no wait — param-source labels the params of public library APIs as sources of taint). Actually it's consistent: for an exported variadic function, the tail args are caller-supplied. Labelling them as entry input matches the named params' treatment. Fine — and it's the JS `__arguments__` precedent exactly (JS exported functions using `arguments` get the synthetic param with exported=true — same loop).\n\nNow the frontend body rewrite for `va_start`/`va_copy`. Where are calls converted in c.go? `c.expr` around line 2696 handles call_expression. I need to find where expression statements are converted and add the assignment. Better: do it at the statement level — when an expression_statement's expression is a call to `va_start`, emit `Assign{Targets:[ap], Value: Name(CVarargsParam)}` BEFORE/AFTER the ExprStmt. Keeping the call itself as ExprStmt preserves the `callPath contains \"va_start\"` context evidence (used by the ruby binding's exemption) and any call_arg facts.\n\nWait — where does `callPath` context come from? Let me check ccFunctionContext — it may collect callee paths of calls in the body. If so, keeping the call is required. Let me check.","signature":"e46b7128cf5b41f9a96846cc"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2e47