{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026091614134663e6408f897d44c0","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The context tokens include the raw body text (`compactCExprText(c.text(body))`) — so `va_start` remains in the body text regardless. But the binding checks `node.context.callPath contains \"va_start\"` — callPath is presumably built from call nodes' callee paths... The tokens like \"call_path:...\" are added by lowering (`functionContextAnalysisEvent`). Since I keep the va_start call in the body (only ADD an assignment), all existing evidence is preserved. Good — additive.\n\nNow let me look at how `c.expr` handles `call_expression`, to find the cleanest interception point for va_start/va_copy. Actually, better to do it in `c.stmt`/`c.exprStmt`: when the statement is `va_start(ap, x);` emit `[ExprStmt(call), Assign(ap, __varargs__)]`. But va_start could also appear... only as a statement in practice. va_copy too. Keep it at the statement level — minimal and safe.\n\nWait, actually — should the assignment target be `ap` (the va_list variable)? In NIR, `Assign{Targets: [\"ap\"], Value: Name{ID: \"__varargs__\"}}`. In lowering, `ap` must already be a declared local (`va_list ap;` → a declaration). How does the C frontend lower `va_list ap;`? `va_list` is a type_identifier; `ap` an identifier. It probably becomes an Assign with no value or a Decl. Then my Assign re-binds `ap`'s scope node to the tail param value — flow: tail param node → ap's node → uses of ap.\n\nLet me now check how the C frontend lowers a plain declaration `va_list ap;` and an assignment `ap = something`. Let me look at the \"declaration\" case in c.stmt.","signature":"c0f7b3d731004b58a19499db"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2e47c8b-b052-45f8-b9f8-606406eb025a","uuid":"84ac03a6-c2b9-4eea-bf7f-390cef832c76","times