{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026091614172882678318cb05487e","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"`selectOverloads` uses `callArity(t, recvNode) == argc` — with the synthetic in paramNames and callArity subtracting it, behaviour matches today exactly.\n\nDecision matrix:\n\n**Synthetic in paramNames (JS style)**:\n- callArity: subtract 1 → matches today's arity (named count). ✔ no overload drift.\n- hasParams/overloadForSignature: paramNames includes synthetic; st.Params includes it → consistent. Today: a variadic decl had paramNames=[named]; after: [named, __varargs__]. For the overload-compare path: two sibling decls of same name — one variadic one not — `f(int)` and `f(int, const char*, ...)`: st.Params for the variadic = [a, b, __varargs__]; cands' paramNames: [a] and [a,b,__varargs__] → exact match picks the right one (better precision than today where [a,b] vs [a] / [a,b]... today the variadic's paramNames=[a,b] and if funcQual picked `f(int)`, hasParams([a],[a,b]) false → overloadForSignature([a,b]) → picks variadic — same outcome).\n- The positional mapping loop maps the FIRST tail arg to the synthetic node via normal indexing; remaining tail args unmapped → my extra loop handles them. Both set mapped.\n- Body scope: setNode via params map — fine.\n\n**Synthetic NOT in paramNames**: hasParams length mismatch → different route, same outcome but fragile.\n\nGo with **in paramNames** (JS precedent). Now `callArity` must subtract BOTH synthetics:\n\n```go\nfunc (l *lowerer) callArity(fi *funcInfo, recvNode string) int {\n\tn := len(fi.paramNames) - l.paramOffset(fi, recvNode)\n\tif _, ok := fi.params[nir.JSArgumentsParam]; ok {\n\t\tn--\n\t}\n\tif _, ok := fi.params[nir.CVarargsParam]; ok {\n\t\tn--\n\t}\n\treturn n\n}\n```\n\nAnd the tail routing loop: first tail index = callArity(target, recvForTargets). Loop `for i := l.callArity(target, recvForTargets); i < len(args); i++ { l.flow(args[i], varargsParam); mapped[i] = true }`. The first tail arg gets flowed twice (once positionally, once here) — dedupe concern: `l.flow` — let me check if it dedupes.\n\nAlso `paramOffset` guard: `len(fi.paramNames) > 0 && fi.paramNames[0] == l.selfName` — C never has selfName first... `l.selfName` = \"this\" default; C params named \"this\"? Practically never. Fine.\n\nOne more place: line 4145 `if l.curClass != \"\" && len(st.Params) > 0 && st.Params[0] == l.selfName` — C has no curClass. Fine.\n\nWhat about `keyword_argument_slot_test.go` / kwarg props — C has no kwargs. Fine.\n\nNow, the `va_list ap;` bare declaration binds `ap` → empty const. My va_start Assign re-binds. Let me also think about a wrapper that forwards the tail to ANOTHER variadic function directly (no va_start), e.g.:\n\n```c\nstatic void log2(const char *fmt, ...) { va_start(ap, fmt); ... }\nstatic void log1(const char *fmt, ...) { log2(fmt, ...); } // impossible in C without va_list\n```\n\nNot possible in C — must use va_start+va_list forwarding. Good.\n\nWhat about the wrapper writing via `vsnprintf(buf, n, fmt, ap)` then `fputs(buf, f)`? ap → vsnprintf args → result → buf → fputs. My change routes tail→ap→vsnprintf's arg2. The `arg→result` conservative edge for vsnprintf (unresolved libc call) flows ap's taint into result → buf → fputs. Works.\n\nNow — what does the definition for rank 3007 actually need? The sink is likely inside the wrapper (`vfprintf`/`vsyslog`/`fprintf`) or the wrapper call itself. With my change, taint reaches INSIDE the wrapper (both the tail Param node and onward through ap to v-calls). If the definition instead labels the wrapper's own call args as sink — that worked before. So the change covers the definition's needs either way.\n\nOne more scenario — PRECISION: could routing tail args into the body create false positives that didn't exist? Yes, in principle: taint entering a wrapper's tail now reaches the wrapper's internal sinks. That's the point (recall), and it's the same semantics named params already have. The review measures benchmarks; this is the intended behaviour change.\n\nAlso consider `printf`-family DEFINITIONS in scanned repos (e.g. a vendored libc implementation defining `int printf(const char *fmt, ...)`) — body opens va_start → synthetic param → calls to printf route their tail args into it. Same class of flow as any wrapper. Fine.\n\nLet me now check `l.flow` dedupes edges.","signature":"b48b64d193a745269fafc2d1"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2e47c8b-b052-45f8-b9f8-606406eb025a","uuid":"d5a6cab4-61c9-4941-bb3b-0dad3dd6e80e","timestamp":"2026-09-16T06:17:50.685Z"}
