{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026091614334069c5fdc3eb6c4b86","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"These are all web apps (Python/JS) — no substantial C repo available for a timing check. The added work per C function definition is one body walk (bounded by body size, same class as the dozens of existing `cc*Observations` walks that already run per function), so the cost profile is in line with existing behaviour. The review measures the heavy corpora anyway.\n\nLet me now do a final code-review pass on my own change with fresh eyes:\n\n1. **`ccWithVariadicTailParam`** — appends synthetic only when body opens va_list. One scenario I should double check: a variadic wrapper whose body opens va_list but where `params` came from the tree and the LAST named param IS the synthetic from a previous... no, fresh conversion.\n\n2. **The lowering loop** — `for i := len(target.paramNames) - paramOffset; i < len(args); i++`. Is `paramOffset` guaranteed such that positional loop covered exactly [0, len-paramOffset)? The positional loop maps `paramIndex = i + paramOffset < len(paramNames)` → i < len(paramNames) - paramOffset. Yes — so my loop starts exactly where positional stopped, and the first tail arg was bound positionally at i = len(paramNames)-1-paramOffset (the synthetic's slot). Correct given the synthetic is LAST — which the frontend guarantees by appending. If some other frontend (only C/C++ emits it) put it elsewhere, the loop would still flow args beyond named into it; only the first-tail positional binding depends on lastness. JS never emits CVarargsParam. OK.\n\n3. **`mapped[i] = true`** — prevents arg→result conservative edge for tail args of va_list-opening wrappers. Intended: the tail now has a real in-body route. \n\n4. **`callArity`** — subtracting the synthetic keeps selectOverloads' arity comparisons identical to pre-change (arity = named count). \n\n5. One more possible interaction: `keyword_argument_slot_test.go` etc. passed. Full suite green.\n\n6. **`estimateStmtListNodes`** — counts len(s.Params)+2; synthetic counted; it's an estimate. Fine.\n\n7. What about **incremental lowering** (`incremental.go`) and the **delta cache** — they hash/compare FuncDef params? If incremental.go has signature-change detection comparing Params, adding the synthetic consistently on re-extraction is stable. Since both old and new modules come from the same binary, consistent. Fine — and full suite (which includes incremental tests) passed.\n\n8. **parsecache** — salt derived from executable; auto-invalidated. Fine.\n\nNow — the earlier graph dump showed `analysis.function.context` calls and a `code.Param __varargs__` with `func=log_msg`. The Param node's `name` prop is `__varargs__` — could that leak into `identifier:` context tokens or bindings matching params by name? `ccFunctionContext` collects `identifier:` tokens from the body TEXT — `__varargs__` doesn't appear in text. Bindings matching Param nodes by name (bindings_index.go:402/464) — a binding naming `__varargs__` would be deliberate. OK.\n\nOne more consideration — the **`vkind=Name`** Arg at vfprintf: `nirKind(a)` for the ap read — the Arg's vkind is Name; binding-side sink matching on arg NIR kind unaffected.\n\nI'm also wondering whether I should double-check the interaction with `ccNarrowCallArgObservations` and other per-function observation walkers that take `params` — they receive `params` (with my synthetic appended?) — look at line 1746ff: `ccDestCapacityUncheckedObservations(n, params)` and `ccUnassignedSizedStackArrayObservations(n, params)` and `ccCursorLoopMissingEndSentinelObservations(n, params)` take params! My synthetic is appended BEFORE those calls (line 1757, before the observation walkers at 1782+). Those walkers index/iterate params — a synthetic trailing name could produce a bogus observation (e.g. \"param __varargs__ has no type\"). Let me check what they do with params.","signature