{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202609161434010ce66a921fde4e23","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_a6fc63fc38104ba0a3652cc4","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"git add internal/extract/nir/nir.go internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/c.go internal/extract/lowering/lowering.go internal/extract/c_variadic_tail_test.go && git commit -m \"$(cat <<'EOF'\nc: trace a variadic call's tail arguments into the wrapper that reads them\n\nA C function declares its variadic tail as `...`, which has no parameter\nname, so the arguments a call passed beyond the named parameters bound to\nnothing: they kept only the conservative argument-to-result edge, which\nnever enters the callee. A printf-style logging wrapper is exactly that\nshape and returns void, so every route from a caller's data to the\nwrapper's own body — where the v-formatted output call lives — was dark,\nand no definition could name a flow through it (rank 3007, the AIDE\ncontrol-character log output, CVE-2025-54389).\n\nThe tail is now modelled the way the JavaScript frontend models\n`arguments`. A definition that declares `...` and opens a va_list over it\ngains a synthetic last parameter, __varargs__; the lowering binds every\nargument past the named parameters to that node (the first positionally,\nthe rest in a tail loop), and va_start binds the list onto it so the\nv-formatted call the list is handed to — and any va_arg read, and a\nva_copy — carries what the caller passed. A variadic body that opens no\nlist reads nothing from its tail and keeps exactly the edges it had, and\ncallArity excludes the synthetic parameter so overload selection is\nunchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEOF\n)\" && git log --oneline -2","description":"Commit the engine change"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2e47c8b