{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202609161434217441f49b937341b9","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Committed. Now the final verification pass — rerun the complete suite one more time on the committed tree to be sure, then write the verdict.\n\nActually I already ran the full suite (`go test ./...` green with defs, spec suite identical failures pre-existing with corpus defs). Let me do one last full run to confirm the committed state, and also double check that `go test ./...` includes cmd/vyql with the defs checkout (it did — zero failures earlier; that run was pre-commit but same tree).\n\nLet me also think about whether the CHANGELOG should be touched — the repo has CHANGELOG.md but it's outside internal/cmd/go.mod — NOT in my allowed paths (only internal/, cmd/, go.mod, go.sum). So no.\n\nOne more consideration for the verdict: blast radius statement.\n\n- Ranks affected beyond 3007: every C/C++ rule whose flow crosses a variadic wrapper that opens a va_list. Concretely in the corpus: definitions that label printf-family format slots (VYQL-INJ-011 / NonliteralFormatString family, e.g. ruby_c_api_format_string) match on call-site facts — unaffected (no dataflow change at the wrapper's own call site... well, the wrapper call's tail args no longer get arg→result edges; a rule that relied on tail taint reaching the wrapper's RESULT would lose that edge. E.g. `x = wrapper(a, secret)` where wrapper opens va_list: previously secret→x (conservative); now secret→body (and x only gets wrapper's return). If some rule relied on that over-approximation, it could lose a finding. The spec suite moved nothing (8264 specs, identical failures), and the full go test suite is green — the measured corpora show no such reliance. But blast radius must name this honestly: tail arguments of va_list-opening wrappers no longer carry the conservative arg→result edge; their taint now enters the body instead. That's the intended trade and the review re-measures every benchmark.\n\n- C++ shares the ccConv walker, so C++ variadic definitions that open va_list gain the same route (template parameter packs are NOT covered — different node kind, unchanged).\n\n- The ruby_c_api binding's va_start exemption (context callPath contains va_start) — I kept the va_start call node, so the evidence survives (verified by the spec suite passing identically).\n\nNow write the verdict file. Schema — let me look at what fields the verdict should have. Is there an example from previous runs? Let me check /campaign/work for other answer files to copy the shape.","signature":"94a56f49100646fd82ffedb2"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2e47c8b-b052-45f8-b9f8-606406eb025a","uuid":"d5793788-c581-486b-a0af-dc3e6538a2f6","timestamp":"2026-09-16T06:34:37.501Z"}
