{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_a13324908d174141b2deda57","type":"tool_result","content":"{\n  \"gap\": \"taint-does-not-cross-a-call-whose-callee-name-is-bound-to-a-\",\n  \"status\": \"DONE\",\n  \"commit\": \"db171821e9508c706ec939911389dd3efb7b4f2f\",\n  \"commits\": [\n    \"db171821e9508c706ec939911389dd3efb7b4f2f\"\n  ],\n  \"branch\": \"engine-work/taint-does-not-cross-a-call-whose-callee-name-is-bound-to-a-\",\n  \"base\": \"a1259023da5f51fce3e6c52b29a32d13b8246858\",\n  \"blocked_ranks\": [\n    2486\n  ],\n  \"changed_files\": [\n    \"internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/javascript.go\",\n    \"internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/javascript_assignment_callee_test.go\"\n  ],\n  \"what_was_missing\": \"internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/javascript.go, (*jsConv).exprStmt, case \\\"assignment_expression\\\". That case already lowered four spellings of \\\"this name names a function\\\" to a nir.FuncDef: module.exports = function..., exports.f = function..., Ctor.prototype.m = function... on an exported constructor, and module.exports = { f: function... }. The declarator case in (*jsConv).stmt (\\\"lexical_declaration\\\", \\\"variable_declaration\\\") lowers a fifth, `const f = (id) => ...`, the same way. A plain identifier target with a function-valued right-hand side was the one spelling left out: it fell through to jsAssignmentExpr and became nir.Assign{Targets: [name], Value: Lambda}, which registers no signature, so (*lowerer).resolveTargets found nothing for the name and the call kept the conservative argument-to-result edge with no edge into any parameter.\\n\\nRank 2486 (vitejs/vite-plugin-rsc, CVE-2025-67489) is blocked on exactly that. Its source shape was re-read from that rank's clone in this session: src/core/rsc.ts:15 `let requireModule!: (id: string) => unknown`, the assignment at src/core/rsc.ts:23 inside `export function setRequireModule(options)`, and the call `await requireModule(file)` at src/core/rsc.ts:70 inside `loadServerAction`. The rank's verdict recorded `vyql graph -call requireModule` showing `Call#314 --FLOWS--> Subscript#315` with no edge into any parameter, and `vyql trace -from HttpInput` reporting all 49 sources dead-ending at src/core/rsc.ts:71. Both reproduce at the base commit in this session.\\n\\nThe layer diagnosis is what makes this the engine's problem rather than a binding's: the definitions cannot express it, because the only text a binding could match at that call site is the local variable's own name, which is a variable name in one repository, not a library API. Rank 2486's verdict says the same and held the rank for it, and its spec's second block is byte-identical to its first except for this one binding spelling.\",\n  \"what_changed\": \"Fifteen lines in internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/javascript.go, one branch in exprStmt's assignment_expression case, placed after the existing member-target branches and before the module.exports-object branch. It fires when the target is a plain identifier and the right-hand side is a function or arrow expression (isJsFuncNode: function_expression, arrow_function, function, generator_function), and it emits the nir.FuncDef the declarator case already emits for the same value -- Name from the target identifier, Params/ParamTypes from the function node with paramsFromFunctionText as the fallback, Body from funcBody, ContextTokens from jsFunctionContext, ParamEntries from jsParamEntries. The nir.Assign is not emitted alongside it, which is what every sibling branch in that case already does.\\n\\nEverything downstream is the existing FuncDef machinery, unchanged: pass 1 registers the name under modkey::name (l.register recurses into function bodies, so an assignment inside a setter registers at module scope), pass 2 lowers the body where the assignment stands, and resolveTargets reaches it through l.funcQual[curModule+\\\"::\\\"+name]. Exported is deliberately left unset -- a bare identifier carries no evidence that it left the module, and the sibling branches that set it are the ones whose syntax says so (module.exports, exports.f, a public-by-convention prototype method).\\n\\nOnly a plain identifier target matches. A member target (`obj.handler = function...`) and a subscript target (`arr[0] = function...`) keep the lowering they have always had; a destructuring target is not an identifier node; a chained assignment (`a = b = fn`) has an assignment_expression as its right-hand side, not a function node, so it is untouched; augmented assignments are a different node kind handled elsewhere.\\n\\nNo other package, no nir node, no cache format, nothing outside internal/. This is the follow-on to gap a-call-whose-callee-is-a-plain-identifier-bound-by-a-const-i (commit 85f06e9a3): that one made a bound name carry the library API's callee PATH, in callee_alias.go; this one makes a bound name carry a callee BODY. They are separate mechanisms and do not interact -- calleeAliasTable reads nir.Assign statements, which this change stops emitting only for function-valued right-hand sides, and an alias of an import is an identifier right-hand side, which this change does not touch.\",\n  \"test\": \"internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/javascript_assignment_callee_test.go, in the package that changed, driving the real tree-sitter JavaScript frontend and then lowering, not hand-built NIR.\\n\\nTestCallThroughAssignmentBoundCalleeReachesAssignedBody uses the repository's own shape -- a module-level `let requireModule` bound inside `setRequireModule(options)` by `requireModule = (id) => { return loaderOptions.load(id) }`, called as `await requireModule(file)` from `loadServerAction(id)`. It asserts the assigned name became a function definition (a code.Param with func=requireModule exists) and that BFS over FLOWS carries loadServerAction's `id` parameter into the assigned body's `id` parameter and into the argument of the `loaderOptions.load(id)` call that body makes.\\nTestCallThroughDeclaringInitialiserCalleeStillReaches is the same module with the loader bound by `const requireModule = (id) => ...`, identical in every name and every intermediate step, asserting the same reachability -- so the two spellings of one binding cannot drift apart again.\\nTestAssignmentBoundCalleeLeavesNonNameTargetsAlone asserts that `registry.handler = (v) => ...` and `registry.slots[0] = (v) => ...` do not become function definitions: those store a function value somewhere, they do not bind a name.\\n\\nRED was verified in THIS session against the base commit, not inherited. The source change was reverted with `git checkout --` while the test file was left in place: TestCallThroughAssignmentBoundCalleeReachesAssignedBody fails with \\\"a name bound to a function by an assignment statement did not become a function definition, so the call through it has no callee body\\\", and the other two PASS, which is what they are for. With the change restored all three pass.\",\n  \"verification\": \"Everything below was run in THIS session against the committed tree at db171821e9508c706ec939911389dd3efb7b4f2f.\\n\\nSuite: VYQL_HOME=/campaign/repos/corpus/vyql go test -count=1 ./... reports ok for every package except cmd/vyql, whose TestVyqlSpecs carries 23 failures against that published corpus. Those 23 are pre-existing and unrelated: the failure list was captured with the change reverted and again with it applied and the two lists are identical apart from timing digits. gofmt -l over the changed package prints nothing and go vet on the treesitter and lowering packages is clean.\\n\\nSpecs against the batch's own definitions (VYQL_HOME=/campaign/work/rank-2486-1/vyql, 8,562 specs from 2,491 files): exactly one moves -- cve_rank2486_vite_rsc_reference_id_dynamic_import.test.vyql, second block, \\\"rule VYQL-INJ-023 fired but should not have\\\". That block is the gap's own record: it asserts the pre-fix silence of the assignment spelling, its first block (the declaring-initialiser spelling) still passes, and the two differ only in the binding this change fixes. Whoever owns vyql/ has to flip that block's reject to an expect; the definitions are not mine to edit.\\n\\nBenchmarks, both gated in CI, re-scored at HEAD on this machine's corpora: BenchmarkJava OVERALL +1.00, BenchmarkPython OVERALL +0.90 -- the standing figures, so neither gate moves and nothing in benchmarks/RESULTS.md or README.md needs changing. Neither was touched. All 22 owasp-* ports were also scored (VYQL_BENCH_ALL_OWASP=1): +1.00 each, PASS, including owasp-js and owasp-typescript, the two corpora this frontend change actually touches.\\n\\nEnd to end on rank 2486's own repository. The clone at /campaign/work/rank-2486-1/repo was left untouched at the fix commit; a copy was taken to /tmp and checked out at the vulnerable parent c8af971f. Binaries built at base and at HEAD, both driven through campaign-scan:\\n  - Finding set over the whole tree is IDENTICAL: 44 findings either way, 0 added, 0 removed (`vyql diff` reports \\\"identical -- finding-set preserved\\\"). Scan wall time 2.6 s either way.\\n  - The edge the gap names is present at HEAD and absent at base. `vyql graph` at base: `Arg#313@rsc.ts:70 --FLOWS--> Call#314 --FLOWS--> Subscript#315` and no requireModule#param node anywhere. At HEAD: `requireModule#param#id code.Param @ src/core/rsc.ts:23` exists, `Arg#312@rsc.ts:70 --FLOWS--> requireModule#param#id`, `requireModule#param#id --FLOWS--> Arg#247` (the options.load call inside the body), and `requireModule#ret# --FLOWS--> Call#313@rsc.ts:70`.\\n  - `vyql trace -from HttpInput -to DynamicCodeLoad`: at base the no-ssr entry.rsc.tsx:24 source dead-ends at src/core/rsc.ts:71. At HEAD it crosses and dead-ends INSIDE the assigned body, at src/core/rsc.ts:24, and reaches further into src/core/shared.ts:13, src/browser.ts:9 and src/core/browser.ts:13.\\n\\nLane: `git diff --name-only a1259023d..HEAD` lists two files, both under internal/. Neither cve-corpus/ledger.tsv nor cmd/vyql/testdata/cve_ledger_digest.tsv exists in this repository. `git status` is clean apart from the harness's own untracked harness.log / harness.err.\",\n  \"blast_radius\": \"Beyond rank 2486:\\n  - Every JavaScript and TypeScript tree in the batch, which is where the idiom lives. A name bound to a function or arrow by an assignment statement -- the setter-wired loader, the conditionally assigned handler, the `let cb; cb = function(){}` callback -- now resolves at call sites that resolved nothing. No measured corpus moved (see verification), so on the corpora available here this is recall at zero observed precision cost, but it is new reachability in trees that were quiet, so the campaign should re-measure the span as it does after any scanner change.\\n  - The 8,562-spec suite moves exactly one spec, the gap's own record at cve_rank2486. Whoever owns vyql/ must flip that block from `reject VYQL-INJ-023` to `expect` for the campaign's coverage check to go green; until then `make specs` in the definitions repository is red by one.\\n  - Recall risk, not precision risk, from name registration. Registering more names grows funcShort, and the unique-name fallback (uniqueTechFuncInfo) refuses a name with two compatible declarations, so a call that used to resolve by unique name can stop resolving if a same-named `x = function(){}` now appears beside it. That is the same behaviour the declarator spelling already has, and no spec or benchmark moved.\\n  - A name assigned different functions on different branches (`if (a) h = f1; else h = f2`) becomes an overload set keyed by argument count; the call resolves to one body rather than both. Same as the declarator spelling.\\n  - No other language. The change is in the JavaScript/TypeScript walker only; the Vue and HTML script extraction routes share that walker and so gain the same behaviour, which is correct -- they are the same language.\\n  - Node ids and the cache formats are unchanged, so fingerprints of existing findings do not move; the whole-tree scan diff on vite-plugin-react is additions-free and removals-free, measured.\",\n  \"considered_and_rejected\": [\n    \"Fixing it in the lowerer instead, by teaching (*lowerer).resolveTargets to look a bare name up in a module-level table of names bound to lambdas and route the call's arguments into the inline lambda's parameters. Rejected on size, not on correctness: it needs a new table built where module scope is entered, a second minting of signature nodes for parameters and returns, and its own answer to the closure question, all of which duplicates what nir.FuncDef already does. The frontend change reuses that path entirely and is fifteen lines. It also leaves the NIR saying what the source says -- the two spellings of one binding lower to the same thing -- rather than encoding an equivalence the lowerer has to re-derive.\",\n    \"Emitting the nir.Assign alongside the FuncDef, so the name keeps its value binding where the assignment stands. Rejected: it lowers the same body twice, doubling nodes and edges at every such assignment, and no sibling branch in that case does it. The value-use case was measured rather than assumed: `let fn; fn = function(v){db.query(v)}; apply(fn, input)` reaches nothing at base and nothing at HEAD, and the declaring-initialiser spelling `const fn = function(v){...}; apply(fn, input)` also reaches nothing, so the change loses no coverage relative to either spelling -- the weakness is shared and pre-existing, not introduced.\",\n    \"Setting Exported on the new FuncDef, on the argument that a module-level name assigned from inside an exported setter is effectively public. Rejected: a bare identifier carries no syntactic evidence of export, Exported is what mints public-API parameter sources, and setting it would widen the source set of every JS/TS tree for a gap that asked only for call resolution. The sibling branches that set it (module.exports, exports.f) are the ones whose syntax says so.\",\n    \"Restricting the branch to module-level assignments, or to names declared at module level, to shrink the blast radius. Rejected: the gap's own evidence has the assignment inside a function body (setRequireModule), so the restriction would not close it, and it would leave the same call resolving differently depending on where in the file the setter happens to sit.\",\n    \"Restricting it to names assigned exactly once per module, mirroring calleeAliasTable's \\\"a name bound more than once is dropped\\\". Rejected as a first move: it needs a pre-pass over the module to count bindings, and the overload machinery already handles multiple declarations of one name the way it handles the declarator spelling. Nothing measured moved, so the extra machinery buys nothing observable; a reviewer who sees a real imprecision from a rebound name should add it as its own narrowing.\",\n    \"Extending callee_alias.go's table to accept !Decl assignments. That file answers a different question -- which library API a bound name stands for -- and its !Decl exclusion is deliberate and tested (\\\"an assignment that is not a declaration binds nothing stable\\\"). This gap is about a callee BODY, which is the FuncDef path's question. Touching callee_alias would have changed callee-path rewriting for every JS/TS module for no reason, and the existing negative test for the assignment spelling still passes unchanged.\",\n    \"A CHANGELOG.md entry. This role may change only internal/, cmd/, go.mod and go.sum; CHANGELOG.md is outside that set and an out-of-lane file is refused. The reasoning lives in the commit message, and a reviewer who wants it in CHANGELOG.md should add it when the pull request lands.\"\n  ],\n  \"reviewer_should_know\": \"What is closed: a call through a name an assignment statement bound to a function value now resolves to that function's body, so its arguments' taint crosses the call instead of terminating there. The two spellings of one binding -- `const f = (id) => ...` and `let f; f = (id) => ...` -- now lower to the same nir.FuncDef and behave identically. Rank 2486's minimal pair confirms it end to end: its reject block, byte-identical to its expect block except for the binding spelling, now reports VYQL-INJ-023 where it was silent.\\n\\nWhat is NOT closed, and is worth saying plainly so nobody mistakes this for the whole CVE: on vite-plugin-react's real source the taint now crosses requireModule(file) and enters the assigned arrow's body, then stops one hop later at `options.load(removeReferenceCacheTag(id))` -- a property of the setter's PARAMETER, which is a separate resolution question this gap does not name. That is why the whole-tree scan diff is 44 = 44 with no new finding. Rank 2486's own spec anticipated exactly this and worked around it in its expect block by holding the options object in a module-level `let loaderOptions` rather than taking it as the arrow's parameter -- its words: \\\"the one substitution a declaration-bound loader needs to stay in scope\\\". With that hop spelled the way the engine can see, the finding fires, and it now fires for both spellings. If the campaign wants the repository's own `options.load` spelling to report too, that is a new gap about property-of-a-parameter dispatch, and it should be raised as one rather than folded in here.\\n\\nThe one judgement to push on is dropping the nir.Assign rather than emitting it beside the FuncDef. It makes the assigned name stop being a value in the scope where the assignment stands, so a function handed onward as a value (`register(fn)`) loses the binding it had. Measured, that case was already dark at base and is dark under the declaring-initialiser spelling too, so nothing is lost relative to the supported spelling -- but it is a behaviour change, it is the same one every sibling branch in that case already makes, and it is the thing most likely to surface as a regression report if some tree relied on it.\\n\\nSecond judgement: the branch has no uniqueness or stability condition. `let h; if (a) h = f1; else h = f2; h(x)` registers two FuncDefs named h and resolves the call to one of them by argument count. That is the same answer the declarator spelling gets for two same-named declarations, and no measured corpus moved, but it is the widest part of the change and the first place to look if a precision report comes in.\\n\\nAction needed outside this lane: cve_rank2486_vite_rsc_reference_id_dynamic_import.test.vyql's second block must change from `reject VYQL-INJ-023` to `expect` in the definitions repository, or `make specs` there stays red by one. That file is not mine to edit.\"\n}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2e47c8b-b052-45f8-b9f8-606406eb025a","uuid":"90b48a7d-9994-4d05-bdc1-92428dc73807","timestamp":"2026-09-16T06:34:41.302Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"{\n  \"gap\": \"taint-does-not-cross-a-call-whose-callee-name-is-bound-to-a-\",\n  \"status\": \"DONE\",\n  \"commit\": \"db171821e9508c706ec939911389dd3efb7b4f2f\",\n  \"commits\": [\n    \"db171821e9508c706ec939911389dd3efb7b4f2f\"\n  ],\n  \"branch\": \"engine-work/taint-does-not-cross-a-call-whose-callee-name-is-bound-to-a-\",\n  \"base\": \"a1259023da5f51fce3e6c52b29a32d13b8246858\",\n  \"blocked_ranks\": [\n    2486\n  ],\n  \"changed_files\": [\n    \"internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/javascript.go\",\n    \"internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/javascript_assignment_callee_test.go\"\n  ],\n  \"what_was_missing\": \"internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/javascript.go, (*jsConv).exprStmt, case \\\"assignment_expression\\\". That case already lowered four spellings of \\\"this name names a function\\\" to a nir.FuncDef: module.exports = function..., exports.f = function..., Ctor.prototype.m = function... on an exported constructor, and module.exports = { f: function... }. The declarator case in (*jsConv).stmt (\\\"lexical_declaration\\\", \\\"variable_declaration\\\") low