TechFuncInfo) refuses a name with two compatible declarations, so a call that used to resolve by unique name can stop resolving if a same-named `x = function(){}` now appears beside it. That is the same behaviour the declarator spelling already has, and no spec or benchmark moved.\\n  - A name assigned different functions on different branches (`if (a) h = f1; else h = f2`) becomes an overload set keyed by argument count; the call resolves to one body rather than both. Same as the declarator spelling.\\n  - No other language. The change is in the JavaScript/TypeScript walker only; the Vue and HTML script extraction routes share that walker and so gain the same behaviour, which is correct -- they are the same language.\\n  - Node ids and the cache formats are unchanged, so fingerprints of existing findings do not move; the whole-tree scan diff on vite-plugin-react is additions-free and removals-free, measured.\",\n  \"considered_and_rejected\": [\n    \"Fixing it in the lowerer instead, by teaching (*lowerer).resolveTargets to look a bare name up in a module-level table of names bound to lambdas and route the call's arguments into the inline lambda's parameters. Rejected on size, not on correctness: it needs a new table built where module scope is entered, a second minting of signature nodes for parameters and returns, and its own answer to the closure question, all of which duplicates what nir.FuncDef already does. The frontend change reuses that path entirely and is fifteen lines. It also leaves the NIR saying what the source says -- the two spellings of one binding lower to the same thing -- rather than encoding an equivalence the lowerer has to re-derive.\",\n    \"Emitting the nir.Assign alongside the FuncDef, so the name keeps its value binding where the assignment stands. Rejected: it lowers the same body twice, doubling nodes and edges at every such assignment, and no sibling branch in that case does it. The value-use case was measured rather than assumed: `let fn; fn = function(v){db.query(v)}; apply(fn, input)` reaches nothing at base and nothing at HEAD, and the declaring-initialiser spelling `const fn = function(v){...}; apply(fn, input)` also reaches nothing, so the change loses no coverage relative to either spelling -- the weakness is shared and pre-existing, not introduced.\",\n    \"Setting Exported on the new FuncDef, on the argument that a module-level name assigned from inside an exported setter is effectively public. Rejected: a bare identifier carries no syntactic evidence of export, Exported is what mints public-API parameter sources, and setting it would widen the source set of every JS/TS tree for a gap that asked only for call resolution. The sibling branches that set it (module.exports, exports.f) are the ones whose syntax says so.\",\n    \"Restricting the branch to module-level assignments, or to names declared at module level, to shrink the blast radius. Rejected: the gap's own evidence has the assignment inside a function body (setRequireModule), so the restriction would not close it, and it would leave the same call resolving differently depending on where in the file the setter happens to sit.\",\n    \"Restricting it to names assigned exactly once per module, mirroring calleeAliasTable's \\\"a name bound more than once is dropped\\\". Rejected as a first move: it needs a pre-pass over the module to count bindings, and the overload machinery already handles multiple declarations of one name the way it handles the declarator spelling. Nothing measured moved, so the extra machinery buys nothing observable; a reviewer who sees a real imprecision from a rebound name should add it as its own narrowing.\",\n    \"Extending callee_alias.go's table to accept !Decl assignments. That file answers a different question -- which library API a bound name stands for -- and its !Decl exclusion is deliberate and tested (\\\"an assignment that is not a declaration binds nothing stable\\\"). This gap is about a callee BODY, which is the FuncDef path's question. Touching callee_alias would have changed callee-path rewriting for every JS/TS module for no reason, and the existing negative test for the assignment spelling still passes unchanged.\",\n    \"A CHANGELOG.md entry. This role may change only internal/, cmd/, go.mod and go.sum; CHANGELOG.md is outside that set and an out-of-lane file is refused. The reasoning lives in the commit message, and a reviewer who wants it in CHANGELOG.md should add it when the pull request lands.\"\n  ],\n  \"reviewer_should_know\": \"What is closed: a call through a name an assignment statement bound to a function value now resolves to that function's body, so its arguments' taint crosses the call instead of terminating there. The two spellings of one binding -- `const f = (id) => ...` and `let f; f = (id) => ...` -- now lower to the same nir.FuncDef and behave identically. Rank 2486's minimal pair confirms it end to end: its reject block, byte-identical to its expect block except for the binding spelling, now reports VYQL-INJ-023 where it was silent.\\n\\nWhat is NOT closed, and is worth saying plainly so nobody mistakes this for the whole CVE: on vite-plugin-react's real source the taint now crosses requireModule(file) and enters the assigned arrow's body, then stops one hop later at `options.load(removeReferenceCacheTag(id))` -- a property of the setter's PARAMETER, which is a separate resolution question this gap does not name. That is why the whole-tree scan diff is 44 = 44 with no new finding. Rank 2486's own spec anticipated exactly this and worked around it in its expect block by holding the options object in a module-level `let loaderOptions` rather than taking it as the arrow's parameter -- its words: \\\"the one substitution a declaration-bound loader needs to stay in scope\\\". With that hop spelled the way the engine can see, the finding fires, and it now fires for both spellings. If the campaign wants the repository's own `options.load` spelling to report too, that is a new gap about property-of-a-parameter dispatch, and it should be raised as one rather than folded in here.\\n\\nThe one judgement to push on is dropping the nir.Assign rather than emitting it beside the FuncDef. It makes the assigned name stop being a value in the scope where the assignment stands, so a function handed onward as a value (`register(fn)`) loses the binding it had. Measured, that case was already dark at base and is dark under the declaring-initialiser spelling too, so nothing is lost relative to the supported spelling -- but it is a behaviour change, it is the same one every sibling branch in that case already makes, and it is the thing most likely to surface as a regression report if some tree relied on it.\\n\\nSecond judgement: the branch has no uniqueness or stability condition. `let h; if (a) h = f1; else h = f2; h(x)` registers two FuncDefs named h and resolves the call to one of them by argument count. That is the same answer the declarator spelling gets for two same-named declarations, and no measured corpus moved, but it is the widest part of the change and the first place to look if a precision report comes in.\\n\\nAction needed outside this lane: cve_rank2486_vite_rsc_reference_id_dynamic_import.test.vyql's second block must change from `reject VYQL-INJ-023` to `expect` in the definitions repository, or `make specs` there stays red by one. That file is not mine to edit.\"\n}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
