{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202609161437173f5682bd18d449a2","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Everything is green on the committed tree:\n- `go test -count=1 ./...` — zero failures (with the defs checkout).\n- Spec suite with the corpus defs: 35 pre-existing failures, identical set before/after (verified by diff).\n- Python and JS graphs byte-identical base vs HEAD.\n- RED verified formally.\n\nOne more check I should do: the memory-ceiling concern for the heaviest corpus. My change adds O(1) nodes per variadic definition that opens a va_list (one Param node), one Assign node set per va_start/va_copy statement, and one FLOWS edge per tail argument at call sites of such wrappers. No new walks beyond one body-scan per function definition (the same class as the ~50 existing observation walks). On a large C tree, printf-style wrappers are a small fraction of functions. The memory delta is bounded by the count of va_start statements — negligible against the existing per-function observation machinery.\n\nAlso let me quantify how common the shape is on some real C code to say something concrete... no local C repo. Fine — the argument stands structurally.\n\nNow write the verdict file. Fields modeled on the sibling:\n\n- gap, status DONE, commit 7e88e6808a (need full sha), commits [full sha], branch, base 39e829d306 (full sha needed), blocked_ranks [3007], changed_files [4 files], what_was_missing, what_changed, test, verification, blast_radius, considered_and_rejected, reviewer_should_know.\n\nLet me get the full SHAs.","signature":"6f1764c8555b4943a30efde1"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2e47c8b-b052-45f8-b9f8-606406eb025a","uuid":"2c598529-6013-48c2-9f16-c73c5aae84fe","timestamp":"2026-09-16T06:37:25.703Z"}
