{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_f7207e9304a74fe1aa8b6a48","type":"tool_result","content":"7e88e6808a512b230445d97aaf26c766de3087b0\n39e829d3067dcd84d2d68be0fceadca0cee62968\n internal/extract/c_variadic_tail_test.go  | 200 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++\n internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/c.go |  88 +++++++++++++\n internal/extract/lowering/lowering.go     |  17 +++\n internal/extract/nir/nir.go               |   6 +\n 4 files changed, 311 insertions(+)","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2e47c8b-b052-45f8-b9f8-606406eb025a","uuid":"ac989ea4-6f53-4a4f-9eed-efc4667648ed","timestamp":"2026-09-16T06:37:26.628Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"7e88e6808a512b230445d97aaf26c766de3087b0\n39e829d3067dcd84d2d68be0fceadca0cee62968\n internal/extract/c_variadic_tail_test.go  | 200 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++\n internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/c.go |  88 +++++++++++++\n internal/extract/lowering/lowering.go     |  17 +++\n internal/extract/nir/nir.go               |   6 +\n 4 files changed, 311 insertions(+)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260916143726d48e55e4b93641c5","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_9ed1eca75db2434f952efa48","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/campaign/work/engine-taint-is-not-traced-into-a-variadic-c-function-call-so-a-pri.answer.json","content":"{\n  \"gap\": \"taint-is-not-traced-into-a-variadic-c-function-call-so-a-pri\",\n  \"status\": \"DONE\",\n  \"commit\": \"7e88e6808a512b230445d97aaf26c766de3087b0\",\n  \"commits\": [\n    \"7e88e6808a512b230445d97aaf26c766de3087b0\"\n  ],\n  \"branch\": \"engine-work/taint-is-not-traced-into-a-variadic-c-function-call-so-a-pri\",\n  \"base\": \"39e829d3067dcd84d2d68be0fceadca0cee62968\",\n  \"blocked_ranks\": [\n    3007\n  ],\n  \"changed_files\": [\n    \"internal/extract/nir/nir.go\",\n    \"internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/c.go\",\n    \"internal/extract/lowering/lowering.go\",\n    \"internal/extract/c_variadic_tail_test.go\"\n  ],\n  \"what_was_missing\": \"A C function declares its variadic tail as `...`, which names no parameter, so the tail arguments a call passed beyond the callee's named parameters bound to nothing. In internal/extract/lowering/lowering.go, (*lowerer).evalCall's per-target loop maps argument i to target.paramNames[i+paramOffset] only while that index is in range; an argument past the end stays unmapped and keeps only the conservative arg->result edge, which never enters the callee. A printf-style logging wrapper is exactly that shape AND returns void, so there was no result for the edge to flow through either: every route from a caller's data into the wrapper's own body -- where the v-formatted output call the CVE's sink sits on lives -- was dark, and no definition could name a flow through it no matter what it said.\\n\\nRank 3007 (aide, CVE-2025-54389, CWE-117: filenames carrying terminal control characters reach report and log output unescaped) is blocked on exactly this. Its flow is scan-derived filename -> the project's variadic log wrapper (log_msg(level, fmt, ...)) -> the v-formatted call inside the wrapper that writes the output. The layer diagnosis is what makes this the engine's problem rather than a binding's: the sink is INSIDE the wrapper's body, and the only text a binding could match at the wrapper's own call site is one project's function name -- a variable name in one repository, not a library API. The definitions cannot express 'cross this call boundary into the body' because the scanner never built the edge.\\n\\nReproduced at the base commit in this session with a probe against the real tree-sitter C frontend plus lowering: for `log_msg(int, const char*, ...)` containing va_start/vfprintf, called as log_msg(1, \\\"scanned %s\\\", name), BFS over FLOWS from the call's third argument slot reaches no argument slot of the vfprintf call, and the callee's only Param nodes are `level` and `format` -- the `...` is dropped by (*ccConv).params because tree-sitter spells it variadic_parameter, which isCParamDecl rejects.\",\n  \"what_changed\": \"The JavaScript frontend's `__arguments__` pattern, applied to the C variadic tail.\\n\\ninternal/extract/nir/nir.go: one new constant, CVarargsParam = \\\"__varargs__\\\", beside JSArgumentsParam, with the gob registration set unchanged (it is a parameter NAME, not a new node kind).\\n\\ninternal/extract/frontend/treesitter/c.go, three additions:\\n  1. (*ccConv).ccWithVariadicTailParam (called from the function_definition case, after all three existing parameter-resolution fallbacks): when the definition's parameter list contains a variadic_parameter node AND the body opens a va_list, append the synthetic parameter. The signature-text fallback's spelling of the tail -- a parameter literally named \\\"...\\\" -- is replaced by the same synthetic so the two resolution paths cannot disagree. A variadic body that opens no va_list reads nothing from its tail and keeps exactly the parameter list it had.\\n  2. (*ccConv).ccBodyOpensVaList plus ccVaStartNames: a body walk that reports whether the body calls va_start or __builtin_va_start (the stdarg.h macro and the compiler builtin it expands to), which is the only way C reads a variadic tail.\\n  3. In (*ccConv).exprStmt's call_expression case, after the existing cPropagators/cReaders handling: va_start(ap, x) additionally emits nir.Assign{ap, Name(__varargs__)} and va_copy(dst, src) emits nir.Assign{dst, Name(src)}, each before the ExprStmt that lowers the call itself. The call node is kept -- its arguments are real reads, and a body that opens a va_list is evidence existing bindings match on (the shipped ruby_c_api_format_string binding exempts wrappers via `node.context.callPath contains \\\"va_start\\\"`; that evidence survives). Binding ap onto the synthetic parameter is what carries the tail: reads of ap -- the v-formatted call's list argument, a va_arg -- evaluate to the parameter node.\\n\\ninternal/extract/lowering/lowering.go, two additions:\\n  1. In (*lowerer).evalCall, after the JSArgumentsParam block in the resolved-target branch: when the target declares the tail parameter, every argument from len(paramNames)-paramOffset onward flows into that node and is marked mapped. The frontend appends the synthetic LAST, so the positional loop above it already binds the first tail argument; this loop binds the rest. The same discipline as a named parameter: the tail now has a real in-body route, so it no longer takes the conservative arg->result edge.\\n  2. (*lowerer).callArity subtracts the synthetic parameter, so overload selection compares call argument counts against named-parameter counts exactly as before.\\n\\nmakeFuncInfo, the body-scope binding and the Param-node minting needed no change: the synthetic goes through st.Params, so the node is created by the existing loop, and the existing `for name, id := range info.params` scope seeding binds __varargs__ inside the body, which is what the va_start Assign's Name resolves against. The parse cache salt is derived from the running executable, so no version bump is needed. Nothing outside internal/ was touched; nothing in vyql/ (which does not exist in this repository) was touched.\",\n  \"test\": \"internal/extract/c_variadic_tail_test.go, in internal/extract (the package that owns the frontend-to-lowered-graph pipeline), driving the real tree-sitter C frontend and then lowering through the existing lowerC helper -- not hand-built NIR. All tests assert BFS over FLOWS from a call-site argument-slot node into a specific argument slot of a call inside the wrapper's body.\\n\\nTestCVariadicTailReachesTheWrapperBody is the gap itself: log_msg(int, const char*, ...) with va_start/vfprintf, called as log_msg(LOG_INFO, \\\"scanned %s\\\", name) -- the third argument reaches the vfprintf's list argument. TestCVariadicTailCarriesEveryTailArgument passes TWO tail arguments and asserts both reach the vsyslog list (the first binds positionally, the second exercises the new tail loop). TestCVariadicTailStaysOffTheFormatSlot is the precision half: a tainted value in the format slot reaches the v-call's format argument and NOT its list argument, and a tail argument reaches the list and NOT the format slot. TestCVariadicTailSurvivesAVaCopy and TestCVariadicTailReachesAVaArgRead pin the two other spellings of a body reading the tail. TestCUnreadVariadicTailKeepsTheResultEdge is the no-drift guard: a variadic function whose body opens no va_list keeps the conservative argument-to-result edge it always had (the tail argument still reaches a sink fed by the call's result).\\n\\nRED was verified in this session, twice: once before implementing (three tests failed, the precision and no-drift guards passed), and once formally against the committed tree -- the three source files were reverted to the base commit with the test file left in place, four gap tests failed with their messages, the sources were restored and all six pass. gofmt -l over the touched packages prints nothing; go vet is clean.\",\n  \"verification\": \"Everything below ran in this session against the committed tree at 7e88e6808a512b230445d97aaf26c766de3087b0.\\n\\nSuite: VYQL_HOME=/campaign/repos/defs/vyql go test -count=1 ./... reports ok for every package, zero failures, including cmd/vyql's TestVyqlSpecs (8,264 specs from 2,387 files) against that definitions tree.\\n\\nSpecs against the shared corpus checkout (VYQL_HOME=/campaign/repos/corpus/vyql, the mid-batch definitions other ranks are moving): 35 failures, and the failure list captured with the change applied is byte-identical to the list captured on a scratch worktree at the base commit 39e829d306 (only wall-clock digits differ). Nothing my change touches. The one initially-alarming case -- cve_rank2044 php-gettext, \\\"a call on a reader held in an object property does not reach the callee\\\" -- is PHP and fails identically at the base commit.\\n\\nNon-C languages are provably untouched: for a Python file and a JavaScript file (the latter using ...rest parameters, the nearest other-language spelling), `vyql graph` output is byte-identical between binaries built at base and at HEAD. Only the C/C++ frontend emits the synthetic parameter, so the lowering additions are no-ops elsewhere.\\n\\nEnd to end through campaign-scan on a two-file C project mirroring the CVE's shape (a variadic log_msg in one file defining va_start/vfprintf, a caller in another file passing a scan-derived name): `vyql graph` shows the complete route including the cross-file call resolution -- report.c's tail Arg#94 --FLOWS--> log.c's log_msg#param#__varargs__ --FLOWS--> the vfprintf call's third argument slot -- while log_msg#param#format flows only to the va_start format read and the vfprintf format slot. The route the rank's definition needs (filename -> the output call inside the wrapper) now exists in the scan graph; the scan reports no findings, which is expected because no CWE-117 definition exists in the checked-out definitions yet.\\n\\nMemory/time: the change adds one body walk per C function definition (the same class as the ~50 existing per-function observation walks, and it returns at the first va_start), one Param node per variadic definition that opens a va_list, one Assign per va_start/va_copy statement, and one FLOWS edge per tail argument at calls to such wrappers. No new node kinds, no cache-format change (the parse cache salt is executable-derived and auto-invalidates), no change to node id minting for anything that existed before. The heaviest-corpus measurement is the review's to make.\",\n  \"blast_radius\": \"Beyond rank 3007:\\n  - Every C and C++ tree in the batch. Any call to a locally-resolved variadic function whose body opens a va_list now routes its tail arguments into that body. That is new reachability in trees that were quiet, so the campaign should re-measure the whole span as it does after any scanner change; on everything measurable here (8,264-spec suite twice, byte-identical non-C graphs) nothing moved.\\n  - The intended trade to watch in review: tail arguments of a va_list-opening wrapper no longer carry the conservative arg->result edge (mapped=true). A rule that relied on a wrapper's RESULT being tainted by its tail -- `x = wrapper(a, secret)` -- loses that over-approximation and gains the in-body route instead. No spec or test measured any reliance on the old edge; the unread-tail case (no va_list) keeps it, pinned by test.\\n  - C++ gains the same behaviour through the shared ccConv walker (a C++ `...` is the same variadic_parameter node). Template parameter packs (`Args... args`) are parameter_pack nodes, NOT variadic_parameter, and are unchanged -- widening to them was rejected as beyond the gap.\\n  - Definitions that key on the wrapper's own call site (call_arg/call_arg_at/call_arg_shape tokens, callee paths) are unaffected: the va_start call node and every argument node the frontend recorded still exist, which is also what keeps the shipped ruby_c_api_format_string va_start exemption working.\\n  - A public (non-static) C variadic definition that opens a va_list gains an `exported` Param node for the tail, so the param-source fallback can label it an entry point exactly as it labels the wrapper's named parameters and JS `__arguments__`. Consistent, but it is a new labelable node.\\n  - No other language. The synthetic parameter exists only where the C/C++ frontend puts it; callArity's subtraction is keyed on the same name and no-ops elsewhere.\",\n  \"considered_and_rejected\": [\n    \"Teaching the taint solver or a binding to flow args into the callee. Rejected on layer: the missing edge is produced at lowering time from what the frontend recorded; a solver-side fix would have to re-derive variadicness from node shapes the graph does not carry, and a binding-side fix cannot cross a call boundary at all -- bindings label nodes, they do not create flow.\",\n    \"Adding the synthetic parameter for every variadic definition regardless of body content. Rejected: it changes call-site behaviour (mapped, no conservative edge) for functions that never read their tail, which is a precision change nobody asked for. Gating on the body opening a va_list makes the presence of the parameter node itself the proof that the tail is read, and leaves every other variadic function's edges exactly as they were.\",\n    \"Keeping the conservative arg->result edge alongside the new in-body route for tail arguments. Rejected: it is not what a named parameter gets, and keeping it would make the wrapper's result tainted by its tail forever, which is precisely the over-approximation this gap exists to replace. The no-drift concern is scoped to the unread-tail case, which keeps the edge and is pinned by TestCUnreadVariadicTailKeepsTheResultEdge.\",\n    \"Rewriting va_start in the frontend as a pure Assign and dropping the call. Rejected: the call's argument reads are real, and shipped bindings match on the wrapper's va_start evidence (ruby_c_api_format_string's `callPath contains \\\"va_start\\\"` exemption). Keeping the ExprStmt and adding the Assign beside it preserves every existing fact additively.\",\n    \"Handling __builtin_va_start/__builtin_va_copy/__va_copy spellings only for va_start. Kept all of them: the cost is three map/switch entries, and freestanding/kernel-style code writes the builtins directly; __va_copy is the old glibc spelling. va_end needs nothing (it reads, writes nothing the body uses after), and va_arg needs nothing (it is an ordinary call whose first argument is a read of the bound list).\",\n    \"Extending detection to C++ template parameter packs. Rejected as beyond the gap: the behaviour named is a C variadic `...`, packs are a different grammar node with different call-site arity semantics, and no rank's evidence names them.\",\n    \"A nir-level test in the treesitter package asserting the Params slice contents, beside the flow tests. Rejected as duplication: the extract-level tests assert the behaviour the gap names (the flow), and a structural assertion on the synthetic name would couple the test to the mechanism rather than the property.\",\n    \"A CHANGELOG.md entry. This role may change only internal/, cmd/, go.mod and go.sum; CHANGELOG.md is outside that set and an out-of-lane file is refused. The reasoning lives in the commit message.\"\n  ],\n  \"reviewer_should_know\": \"What is closed: the tail arguments of a call to a locally-resolved C/C++ variadic function whose body opens a va_list now flow into that body -- through the synthetic __varargs__ parameter, onto the va_list that va_start binds, and from there to the v-formatted call the list is handed to, to any va_arg read, and through va_copy. Rank 3007's shape (filename -> variadic log wrapper -> output call inside the wrapper) is traced end to end, including the cross-file case where the wrapper is defined in a different translation unit than the caller, which the unique-tech-name resolution already handled for named functions.\\n\\nWhat is NOT closed, so nobody mistakes this for the whole CVE: the engine now builds the route; the finding still requires the rank's own definition to label a source (the scan-derived filename) and a sink (the output call) and to pass the fixed-revision differential. That work is the rank's, in the definitions repository. Two adjacent engine limits remain and are deliberate non-goals: a va_start hidden behind a project macro is not recognized (the frontend reads source text, not preprocessed output), so such a wrapper keeps its previous conservative behaviour; and a wrapper whose body forwards its va_list to another function taking va_list by pointer gets no route through that helper unless the helper's own reads of the list are arguments of calls the lowering can see -- the va_copy binding covers the common spelling.\\n\\nThe one judgement to push on in review is mapped=true for tail arguments: it removes the conservative arg->result edge for exactly the calls that gained the in-body route. That is the same contract a named parameter already has (an in-body transform is honoured, an ignored argument is dropped), it is why the change improves precision rather than only recall, and it is guarded by the unread-tail test -- but it is the first place to look if a benchmark reports a lost detection through a variadic wrapper's return value.\\n\\nNo action is needed outside this lane: no spec in the definitions repository encodes the old silence (the 35 corpus-checkout failures are pre-existing and unrelated; the matched defs tree is fully green), so unlike some previous engine gaps there is no reject-to-expect flip waiting on the definitions owner.\"\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2e47c8b-b052-45f8-b9f8-606406eb025a","uuid":"0d28499e-4e42-4b3a-a6d5-df294c8234a7","timestamp":"2026-09-16T06:38:34.207Z"}
