{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202609161440410062ae665daa4350","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verdict validated — parses clean, `changed_files` matches the commit exactly, branch and base check out. The gap is closed and landed.\n\n## Summary\n\n**Status: DONE** — commit `7e88e6808a` on `engine-work/taint-is-not-traced-into-a-variadic-c-function-call-so-a-pri` (base `39e829d306`), verdict written to `/campaign/work/engine-taint-is-not-traced-into-a-variadic-c-function-call-so-a-pri.answer.json`.\n\n**What was missing.** A C variadic tail (`...`) names no parameter, so in `(*lowerer).evalCall` an argument past the callee's named parameters bound to nothing and kept only the conservative arg→result edge — which never enters the callee. A printf-style logging wrapper is exactly that shape *and* returns void, so there was no result to flow through either: every route from a caller's data into the wrapper's body, where the v-formatted output call lives, was dark. Reproduced at base with a probe against the real tree-sitter C frontend: `variadic_parameter` was dropped by `isCParamDecl`, and no FLOWS path crossed the boundary. Rank 3007 (aide, CVE-2025-54389) is blocked on precisely this, and no binding could fix it — bindings label nodes, they don't create flow across a call boundary.\n\n**What changed** (4 files, all in lane):\n- `internal/extract/nir/nir.go` — one constant: `CVarargsParam = \"__varargs__\"`, the JS `__arguments__` pattern applied to C.\n- `internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/c.go` — append the synthetic tail parameter when a `variadic_parameter` exists *and* the body opens a va_list (unread tails keep exactly their old edges); emit `Assign{ap, __varargs__}` for `va_start` and `Assign{dst, src}` for `va_copy`, keeping the call nodes so existing `va_start` evidence (e.g. the shipped `ruby_c_api_format_string` exemption) survives.\n- `internal/extract/lowering/lowering.go` — route tail args into the synthetic param in `evalCall`; subtract it in `callArity` so overload selection is unchanged.\n- `internal/extract/c_variadic_tail_test.go` — 6 tests through the real frontend + lowering.\n\n**What the test proves.** RED verified twice (pre-implementation, and formally by reverting the 3 source files against the committed tests: 4 failures, then all 6 green). The gap test pins the full route — the tail arg at the wrapper's call site reaches the `vfprintf` list slot inside the body. Precision is pinned too: the format slot and tail slot don't bleed into each other. The no-drift guard pins that a variadic body which opens no va_list keeps its conservative arg→result edge.\n\n**Verification.** `go test -count=1 ./...` fully green with the matched defs (8,264 specs). Corpus-checkout failures (35) diffed base vs HEAD: byte-identical lists — pre-existing, unrelated. Python and JS scan graphs byte-identical base vs HEAD. End-to-end through `campaign-scan` on an AIDE-shaped two-file project: the cross-file route `report.c` tail Arg → `log_msg#param#__varargs__` → `vfprintf` list slot exists in the scan graph.\n\n**Blast radius beyond rank 3007:** every C/C++ tree in the batch — calls to va_list-opening wrappers gain in-body reachability that was previously quiet (re-measure the span as usual; nothing measurable moved). The one trade to watch in review: tail args of such wrappers no longer carry the conservative arg→result edge (`mapped=true`) — same contract as a named parameter, guarded by the unread-tail test, but the first place to look if a benchmark loses a detection through a variadic wrapper's return value. C++ template packs are deliberately untouched (different grammar node, beyond the gap). No other language is affected.\n\n**Considered and rejected** (8 items in the verdict, headline cases): solver-side or binding-side fixes (wrong layer — the edge is built at lowering time); unconditional synthetic params for all variadic definitions (precision change nobody asked for); keeping the old conservative edge alongside the new route (would keep the wrapper's result tainted by its tail forever — the over-approximation this gap exists to replace); C++ template packs (beyond the gap)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b2e47c8b-b052-45f8-b9f8-606406eb025a","uuid":"85cb158e-8ddd-4613-8914-620d1bb17413","timestamp":"2026-09-16T06:40:58.556Z"}
